          bodyZh: `7.6 The causes of language change
· Linguists have little idea about what causes a language to change.
· Some changes are more obvious and quick (lexical system); others are more gradual (phonological and syntactic systems).
· Reasons for some changes are relatively obvious:
– Rapid development of science and technology has led to creation of many new words: bullet train, fax, laser printer, hi-tech, CD-ROM, laptop computer, iphone.
– Social and political changes and political needs supply new words: shuttle diplomacy, mini-summit, jungle war, Euro, Scientific Outlook on Development.
– More women taking up activities formerly reserved for men → more neutral job titles: chairperson, supervisor, police officer, firefighter, jury foreperson.
· Other changes are more difficult to explain (e.g., no one knows exactly why vowel shift took place).
· The way children acquire a language provides a basic cause of change.
– Children develop language without instruction; early simple grammars become more complex.
– Children's grammar is never exactly like that of the adult community.
– Children receive input from many dialects and individual styles; features may merge.
– Certain rules may be simplified or overgeneralized.
· The older generation may use "variable" rules (e.g., "It's I" and "It's me"); the next generation may use only the "me" form → grammar changed.
· Another cause: "economy of memory", resulting in grammar simplification.
– Many exceptional morphemes lost their special status due to "linguistic laziness".
– This is called internal borrowing or analogical change.
– Example: by analogy to foe/foes, dog/dogs, speakers started saying cows instead of earlier plural kine.
– Regularization of exceptional plural forms: agendums, curriculums instead of agenda, curricula; dwarfs instead of dwarves.
· "Theory of least effort": cheap used in place of cheaply.
– In theory, cheap is adjective, cheaply is adverb.
– Increasingly, cheap is used as an adverb, especially colloquially: "Cathy got it cheap."
– Some young speakers refuse "Cathy got it cheaply."
– Possible that cheaply will disappear; same form will function as both adjective and adverb (like better).
· Simplification of grammar occurs, so does elaboration or complication.
– When English lost most inflectional endings, strict word-order constraints were placed on grammar.
– Countertendency: desire to be intelligible.
– Loss of morphological rule creating causative verbs from adjectives (e.g., blacken from black) did not lose notion of causation; can say "to cause to be red."
– Languages maintain a balance between expressibility and grammatical complexity.
· No single cause of language change; many factors contribute: lexical addition, borrowing, simplification of grammars, elaboration, etc.
· Linguistic change is complex rather than simple to describe; exact reasons still elusive and need further investigation.`,
