          bodyZh: `6.3 Principle of conversation
· In conversation, people do not always express themselves literally; sometimes they use implications.
· Example:
– A: What's the time?
– B: It's ten o'clock. (explicit)
– B: The bell has just rung. (implied; A infers the time based on common knowledge)
· Paul Grice specified two kinds of implied meaning or implicature:
– Conventional implicature: based on the conventional meaning of certain words.
◦ Example: "He is rich but he is not greedy." implies rich people are usually greedy; derived from "but".
– Non-conventional implicature: Grice singled out particularized conversational implicature, inferred by the hearer with reference to the context.
◦ Example: A: Where is the steak? B: The dog looks very happy. (implicature: the dog has eaten the steak)
· Cooperative Principle (CP):
– In Grice's view, participants must be willing to cooperate; otherwise conversation would not be possible.
– CP: Make your conversational contribution such as is required, at the stage at which it occurs, by the accepted purpose or direction of the talk exchange in which you are engaged.
· Four maxims under CP:
– The maxim of quantity:
◦ 1. Make your contribution as informative as is required (for the current purpose of the exchange).
◦ 2. Do not make your contribution more informative than is required.
– The maxim of quality:
◦ 1. Do not say what you believe to be false.
◦ 2. Do not say that for which you lack adequate evidence.
– The maxim of relation: Be relevant.
– The maxim of manner:
◦ 1. Avoid obscurity of expression.
◦ 2. Avoid ambiguity.
◦ 3. Be brief (avoid unnecessary prolixity).
◦ 4. Be orderly.
· Conversation participants nearly always observe the CP, but they do not always observe these maxims strictly.
· Maxims can be violated for various reasons: to mislead, lack of information, not wanting to continue, etc. Such violations do not generate implicature.
· Only when a maxim is "flouted" (violated blatantly, both speaker and hearer aware) does "conversational implicature" occur.
· Flouting a maxim makes language indirect.
· Examples of flouting and resultant implicatures:
– Flouting quantity: A: When is Susan's farewell party? B: Sometime next month. (B knows exactly; implicature: I do not wish to tell you.)
– Flouting quality: A: Would you like to join us for the picnic on Sunday? B: I'm afraid I have got a class on Sunday. (B knows false; implicature: I do not want to join you.)
– Flouting relation: A: How did the math exam go today, Jonnie? B: We had a basketball match with the other class and we beat them. (irrelevant; implicature: I don't wish to talk about the math exam.)
– Flouting manner: A: Shall we get something for the kids? B: Yes. But I veto I-C-E-C-R-E-A-M. (spelling out; implicature: I don't want the kids to know we are talking about ice cream.)
· Significance of Grice's CP: explains how it is possible for the speaker to convey more than is literally said.`,
