          bodyZh: `6.4 Cross-cultural pragmatic failure
· Communication breakdowns sometimes occur. Causes:
– Structure of the language itself.
– Speaker fails to communicate effectively to the hearer.
– Hearer fails to see the speaker's intention.
· Examples:
– Passenger: Can you tell me when we get to Birmingham, please? Driver: It's a big place. You won't miss it. (different interpretations of the when-clause)
– Hostess: It's really been a long day, Leo. I think Steve can give you a lift back home. Guest: Oh, I'm fine. Can I have another cup of coffee, please? (guest fails to see hostess's intention to end the evening)
– Chinese learner: Have you had your lunch, Professor Higgins? Professor Higgins: ... (surprised; inappropriate greeting in English culture)
· Pragmatic failure: occurs when the speaker fails to use language effectively to achieve a specific communicative purpose, or when the hearer fails to recognize the intention or the illocutionary force of the speaker's utterance in the context of communication.
· More likely to occur in cross-cultural communication because different languages differ not just in form but also in use.
· Two dimensions of pragmatics:
– Pragmalinguistics: the more linguistic end of pragmatics; how linguistic forms of a language are used to serve specific pragmatic purposes.
– Sociopragmatics: the sociological interface of pragmatics; customary ways in which people of a particular culture behave to achieve a particular purpose.
· Two kinds of pragmatic failure:
– Pragmalinguistic failure: transferring a form in one's own language to a foreign language, wrongly assuming it performs the same communicative function.
◦ Example: Chinese "没关系" as response to thanks -> "It doesn't matter" in English.
◦ Example: "Have you had your lunch?" as a greeting.
– Sociopragmatic failure: differences in the customary ways of behaving.
◦ Example: Responding to compliments by denying the truth (Chinese modesty vs. English acceptance).
◦ Example: Asking personal questions (age, income, marital status) to English speakers.
· To familiarize ourselves with the pragmatics of the foreign language is of essential importance; learning a language includes learning its rules of use to avoid pragmatic failure.`,
