          bodyZh: `6.5 Hyponymy
· Definition
– Relation of inclusion.
– Meaning of more specific word included in that of another more general word (Lyons 1977, Jackson 1988).
· Examples
– tulip and rose are hyponyms of flower.
– lion and elephant are hyponyms of animal.
· Terms
– General words: flower, animal = superordinate terms (or hyperonym).
– More specific words: tulip, rose, lion, elephant = subordinate terms (or hyponym).
· Unilateral implication
– If it is a rose then it is a flower, but not necessarily vice versa.
– If implication bilateral, then synonymy:
◦ egg-plant and aubergine: if egg-plant then aubergine, and vice versa.
· Hyponymous sets
– hammer, saw, screwdriver, spanner, etc. under tool.
– plaice, cod, herring, sole, etc. under fish.
· Taxonomies
– Relationship creates taxonomies or tree-like configurations.
– Higher-order superordinates above lower subordinates.
– Status as superordinate or subordinate depends on words related to.
– horse, dog, snake: subordinates in relation to animal; superordinates with reference to mare, hound, cobra.
– animal becomes hyponym of creature, which is hyponym of living things.
– Whole vocabulary could be hierarchically organized.
· Pseudo-hyponymy
– Other more lax types important in language.
– Implication rules do not necessarily apply.
– Basic way of creating taxonomy-like relationship within fields corresponding to social or psychological concepts.
– watches, ties, cameras as presents in particular contexts.
– Statements like "watches, ties, cameras and other presents" without permanent implication.
· Quasi-hyponymy
– Sometimes no item of same grammatical class to act as superordinate.
– round, square, oblong: use noun shape as pseudo-superordinate.
– taste serves as superordinate for bitter, sweet, sour.
· Helpfulness in language processing
– Receptive processing (reading comprehension)
◦ Cohesion by hyponymy important key.
◦ Example: "There was a fine rocking-chair that his father used to sit in, a desk where he wrote letters, a nest of small tables and dark, imposing bookcase. Now all this furniture was to be sold, and with it his own past."
◦ Hyponyms under furniture give writing coherence and key to understanding.
◦ Superordinate not necessarily immediate; general word:
▪ items, objects, things
▪ General superordinates covering human and abstract areas: people, creature, idea, fact.
– Productive processing (writing and speaking)
◦ Knowing semantic features of hyponyms and superordinates helps achieve vividness, exactness, concreteness, definiteness.
◦ Compare:
▪ ① "Trees surround the water near our summer place."
▪ ② "Old elms surround the lake near our summer cabin."
▪ ① "I met a writer who is the relation of a politician."
▪ ② "I met a newspaper reporter who is the brother of Senator Buckley."
◦ In each pair, ② better because uses subordinates, concrete and precise, vivid verbal picture.
◦ ① uses superordinates, general and vague.
– Note
◦ Subordinates not always better than superordinates.
◦ Good writing often involves both working together.`,
