          bodyZh: `6.6 Semantic Field
· Definition
– Massive word store conceived of as composed around number of meaning areas.
– Some large: "philosophy", "emotions".
– Others smaller: "kinship", "colour".
– Viewing totality of meaning in compartmentalized way is basis of field theory.
· Trier (1930s)
– Wrote of meaning fields as "living realities intermediate between individual words and the totality of the vocabulary" (quoted in Lyons 1977).
– Saw vocabulary as "an integrated system of lexemes interrelated in sense".
· Basic idea
– "Words of language can be classified into sets which are related to conceptual fields and divide up the semantic space or semantic domain in certain ways" (Lehrer 1974).
· Examples
– apple, pear, peach, apricot, date, mango, pineapple, orange, lemon: semantic field of "fruit".
– celery, lettuce, leek, cucumber, potato, spinach, tomato, egg-plant, carrot: field of "vegetable".
– red, orange, yellow, green, white, black, blue, purple, pink: field of "colour".
· Hierarchy of fields
– Whole vocabulary divided into fields joined together to form fields of higher order.
– Until entire vocabulary included.
– Roget's Thesaurus: uses scheme of universal concepts as framework, lists words sharing same concept.
– Longman Lexicon of Contemporary English: describes some 15,000 items, categorized into fourteen semantic fields of pragmatic everyday nature.
◦ Each semantic field consists of many sub-fields.
◦ Under "family relations": father, mother, son, daughter, brother, sister.
◦ Under "other family relations": uncle, aunt, nephew, niece, cousin.
◦ Field concerning "family in the past or future": ancestor, forefather, forebear, descendant, generation.
· Interdependence of words
– Words organized into fields where interdependent and define one another.
– Meaning does not reside in word itself but spreads over neighbouring words.
– Neighbours identify semantic field and help pin down meaning.
– rose: operates in contrast with tulip and dahlia in one semantic field → flower.
– In contrast with red and purple in another → kind of colour.
– captain: "He is a captain" not very meaningful until know semantic field.
◦ Merchant service: subordinate mate or first officer.
◦ Navy: subordinate commander.
◦ Army: subordinate lieutenant.
◦ (Quirk 1978:139)
· Constant change
– Vocabulary in constant change; old items drop out, new come in.
– Internal relations of whole set alter.
– Example: personal address system in modern English.
◦ Decades ago:
▪ Female: unmarried Miss, married Mrs, unknown —
▪ Male: Mr
◦ Now:
▪ Female: unmarried Miss, married Mrs, unknown Ms
▪ Male: Mr
◦ (Based on Carter and McCarthy 1988:20)
· Cross-linguistic differences
– Semantic field of same concept may not have same members in different languages.
– Kinship terms example:
◦ English uncle covers:
▪ father's younger brother
▪ father's elder brother
▪ father's sister's husband
▪ mother's sister's husband
▪ mother's brother
◦ Chinese has separate terms.
◦ English aunt, cousin, nephew, niece similarly broad.
◦ Poses problem in language learning.
◦ Chinese learners puzzled over exact referent of uncle, aunt, cousin.
◦ Even brother and sister, including elder brother and younger brother, elder sister and younger sister, become riddle.
◦ Comparative study of semantic fields helpful in foreign language learning.`,
