          titleZh: "原文要点",
          bodyZh: `5.4 Componential Analysis
· Definition
– Approach to analyze word meaning by breaking it down into semantic features or components.
– Also called semantic feature analysis.
· Basic idea
– Word meanings can be described in terms of semantic features.
– Features are abstracted from words.
– Called semantic features, components of sense of words.
· Binary terms
– Use of same term with "+" and "−" signs.
– Indicate whether a word has feature (+) or opposite feature (−):
◦ +ADULT (adult)
◦ −ADULT (young)
◦ +MALE (male)
◦ −MALE (female)
◦ +HUMAN (human)
◦ −HUMAN (non-human)
· Examples
– man: +HUMAN, +ADULT, +MALE
– woman: +HUMAN, +ADULT, −MALE
– boy: +HUMAN, −ADULT, +MALE
– girl: +HUMAN, −ADULT, −MALE
· Neutralization
– Sex dimension of meaning of many lexical items is neutralized.
– Examples: cat, child
– Mark used: "±" or "O"
– Leech uses "O":
◦ child: +HUMAN, −ADULT, OMALE
◦ cat: −HUMAN, +ADULT, OMALE
· Defining features
– Focus on features that distinguish one word from another.
– Examples:
◦ horse, cattle vs. machine, air: [+ANIMATE]
◦ road, house vs. thought, philosophy: [+CONCRETE]
◦ water, gas vs. stone, tree: [+COUNTABLE]
– Verbs also have defining features:
◦ [+MOMENTARY], [+DYNAMIC], [+TRANSITIVE]
· Advantages
– (1) Demonstrates relations of meaning between words
◦ Knowing semantic features helps grasp conceptual meaning.
◦ dog: [+ANIMATE, −HUMAN, +CANINE, +DOMESTIC] plus [OMALE] or [+COUNTABLE]
◦ foal: [+ANIMATE, −HUMAN, +EQUINE, −ADULT, OMALE] = "young horse"
– (2) Shows synonymy
◦ Gives two items same componential features.
◦ spinster and maiden: both [+HUMAN, −MALE, −MARRIED]
◦ Differ in connotations: spinster negative, maiden positive.
– (3) Tells whether collocation or syntactic structure is acceptable
◦ Example 1:
▪ * "He has left this neighbourhood for ten years."
▪ "He has lived in this neighbourhood for ten years."
▪ leave: [+MOMENTARY], not co-occur with "for+time" ([-MOMENTARY]).
▪ live: [−MOMENTARY], compatible with "for+time".
◦ Example 2:
▪ "The robbers broke into the bank with a hammer."
▪ * "A hammer broke into the bank by the robbers."
▪ break: [+DYNAMIC], entails subject [+ANIMATE].
▪ robber: [+HUMAN], included in [+ANIMATE].
▪ hammer: [−ANIMATE], incapable of being agent.
· Demerits and controversy
– (1) Limited scope
◦ Well applicable to concrete words with definite referents.
◦ Not effective for abstract ideas or unclear referents.
◦ Examples: beauty, delight, hostility—hard to abstract components.
◦ Even concrete game hard to analyze.
◦ Wittgenstein: know what game means only by recognizing "family resemblance".
◦ Labov's experiment with "cup": often word cannot be defined in terms of clear-cut, unvarying set of features.
– (2) Not enough to break down sense into physical aspects of referents
◦ Many words used figuratively.
◦ Example: tiger defined as [+ANIMATE, +FELINE, +CARNIVOROUS], but does not explain:
▪ "There is a mixture of the tiger and the ape in the character of the imperialists."
◦ tiger here = "cruel and ferocious".
◦ Figurative use allows impossible collocations:
▪ beautiful tyrant, cold fire, honorable villain
◦ Oscar Wilde, The Ballad of Reading Gaol:
▪ "With yawning mouth the yellow hole / Gaped for a living thing; / The very mud cried out for blood / To the thirsty asphalt ring;"
▪ yawning mouth, gaped with hole; mud cried out; thirsty asphalt—impossible in literal sense.
▪ [−ANIMATE] things personified, endowed with human qualities.
· Conclusion
– Usefulness limited to certain extent.
– In actual language use, need to employ in combination with other techniques.`,
